hey there my name is raine harasaki if  you haven't heard of me i'm an american  breath of the wild player who has spent  over 2 300 hours battling in hyrule  i spent a whole lot of time making  combat videos and pioneering several  breath of the wild techniques  the bowspin for example is one of my  creations so naturally when a game came  along that everyone was touting as a  breath of the wild clone  my interest was piqued and i had to see  what it was like for myself  since its first reveal gentian impact  got both a lot of attention and flack  for its breath of the wild like  appearance and gameplay  making people question its originality  why would i play this game if it's just  the worst version of breath of the wild  they would say after playing this game  for 50 hours i can confirm that yes  it does have similarities to breath of  the wild but that doesn't mean you  shouldn't play it  and there's a lot of interesting things  going on with this game which i'll go  over  to make it easier to digest the  comparison can be split into three main  sections  exploration combat and overall thoughts  this is where i felt gentian impact is  the most similar to breath of the wild  and this is also what was shown most in  trailers leading up to genji  impact's release which made people angry  to the point of destroying ps4s in  protests of this game's development  when it comes to exploration genji  impact really feels like a bit of a  mixed bag  the similarities are obvious to the  point of being blatant the gliding  mechanics are pretty much the same  and both games have the options to climb  or jump up most surfaces  jump off walls attack while falling and  use stamina to sprint and much more  in that respect gentian impact feels  really familiar and it's easy to get  accustomed to the exploration when you  know exactly what you're doing  at times it can feel alarmingly similar  particularly when you reach parts of the  map such as mount hulau and cape oath  which are nearly identical to satori  mountain and cave kales from breath of  the wild  taking inspiration from a game as well  received as breath of the wild is  understandable and honestly more games  should take inspiration from it  but i did begin to wonder how gentian  impacts developers were able to get away  with this level of similarity  genji impact even keeps some of breath  of the wild issues such as automatically  clambering onto objects you didn't mean  to  but to its credit gen chin also  streamlines some things from breath of  the wild gameplay system such as fast  swimming instead of breath of the wild  swim dash  option which limited your ability to  steer link mercifully you can also climb  in the rain in genjen this makes it much  less stressful to traverse when the  weather isn't ideal  even though for the most part people  dislike the rain climbing limitations  difficulties such as these would make  you think more about how to approach  traversal and whether there's other ways  around  in many cases it pushes players into  other mechanics such as camping until  the weather clears  using rivali's gale and other  unconventional methods that can present  alternate ways to get to places  one of the wonders of breath of the wild  is its playground-like world and its use  of multiple tools to manipulate that  world  that's what made it engrossing to be in  while gentian impact doesn't really have  those elements i don't think it's  entirely necessary  as its developers really focus on making  an rpg first  and an open world adventure game second  and that's okay  one thing gen chin does right just like  breath of the wild is the breadcrumb  like exploration  the fact that you can get distracted so  easily when you're going from place to  place makes gangshin impact's world  extremely fun to explore  and makes it rewarding to go out of your  way to scavenge materials on your way to  your next objective  and while the ubisoft tower system is  nothing new or exclusive to breath of  the wild  it's an integral part of exploration  that makes an appearance in gangshin as  well  so is gentian impact's exploration as  good as breath of the wild  exploration not really it's perhaps more  intuitive but not as considered  either way you will definitely get lost  in it and probably enjoy the vast  colorful world  the fact that you get to have that in a  game that's played for free is a huge  plus in my book  my definition of breath of the wild  combat is going to be completely skewed  compared to the average player so i'll  try to approach this from a top down  view in combat in general  i can safely say that this work engine  impact deviates the most from breath of  the wild  and it is immensely fun to play in its  own distinct way while breath of the  wild did heavily use elements in combat  gangshin impact uses them differently  rewarding character swapping to gain  buffs and encouraging element mixing  this is done  by tying each character to a specific  element and when combined with weapons  and artifacts effects it really creates  satisfying depth  this is where the rpg element really  shines and makes elemental play  completely different in gangshin impact  to go a little deeper general combat  overall is fairly different in genji as  well  besides one counter stance character  there's no proper guarding or parry  button against an impact  leaving dash is your only defensive  maneuver and while there are falling  attacks you're unable to do any action  while jumping  making most jump cancels in the game  fairly useless the control scheme in  genchin is also completely different but  it feels good to perfectly dodge an  attack and then blast mixed elements  against your foes  all in all both games are fun in their  own independent way and have rewarding  combat breath of the wild just has a  kind of room for creativity that appeals  to lab monsters like me  the leveling of multiple characters and  progression of artifacts and weapons and  gangshin impacts is a fun system that  gives the game a unique flavor that  channels its gotcha game roots  while acquiring these characters feels  pretty unnatural and completely breaks  the story immersion of the game  the characters are actually all very  unique and all of them can be viable on  a team  except for amber having this character  swapping system  almost makes me want a similar system of  switching characters with zelda and link  in the breath of the wild sequel  but ultimately i think that would feel  unnatural and would break the immersion  of a mainline zelda game  gen impact like i stated before is an  rpg first  in an open world adventure game second  as a person who loves both genres very  much this marriage really does work out  in this case  there are a ton of rpgs out there that  kill the exploration factor with design  decisions such as invisible barriers and  streamlined dungeon crawls that go in a  singular path  and genchin doesn't do that while the  story isn't much to write home about  even when compared to breath of the  wild's rather light story the nice  visual aesthetic  the in-depth combat and the  breadcrumb-like exploration  really creates a sense of immersion and  while yes genji  impacts breath of the wild inspirations  border on outright copying  that shouldn't inspire anger and  objections why wouldn't you want more  games to take inspiration from one of  the greatest games of all time  and remember if you're turned off by  genji's gotcha elements this game is  completely free  with that in mind it makes it hard not  to recommend at least trying this game  out  at the very least it's a good game to  keep you busy until age of calamity and  the breath of the wild sequel shows up  whenever that is and when it does you'll  find me here exclusively on gamespot for  breath of the wild combat content  you can be sure i'll be digging very  deep into that game and if you want to  see more gentian impact content let us  know in the comments below  thanks for watching  [Music]  you
 

